      "counts": {"original_exercises": 10, "exercise_tasks": 41, "raw_prose_blocks": 25, "prose_mathematics_checks": 6},
      "prose_mathematics": [
        {
          "id": "n46-prose-1",
          "source_refs": ["p0273#12", "p0274#1", "p0274#2", "p0274#3", "p0274#4"],
          "derivation": "5x^2-4x-12=(5x+6)(x-2). Roots -6/5 and 2; sum 4/5 and product -12/5.",
          "conclusion": "The introductory example and coefficient interpretation are correct."
        },
        {
          "id": "n46-prose-2",
          "source_refs": ["p0274#5", "p0274#6", "p0274#7"],
          "derivation": "For a != 0 and D=b^2-4ac, (2ax+b)^2=D. Thus the two real roots for D>0 are (-b-sqrt(D))/(2a) and (-b+sqrt(D))/(2a). Their sum is -b/a; their product is (b^2-D)/(4a^2)=c/a.",
          "raw_error": "p0274#5 faithfully contains sqrt(b) in x_2. It is wrong: for 5x^2-4x-12, b=-4 while D=256, so sqrt(b) is not real and sqrt(D)=16 gives x_2=2.",
          "required_corrected_token": "\\sqrt{D}",
          "conclusion": "sqrt(b) -> sqrt(D) is a genuine quadratic-formula erratum, not a discretionary modernization. Raw must remain unchanged."
        },
        {
          "id": "n46-prose-3",
          "source_refs": ["p0274#8", "p0275#1", "p0275#2", "p0275#3", "p0275#4", "p0275#6"],
          "derivation": "For D=0 the only distinct root is -b/(2a), counted twice. Then 2x=-b/a and x^2=b^2/(4a^2)=c/a. For a=1 the sum and product become -b and c. The source's preceding real-root convention conditions the theorem.",
          "conclusion": "The repeated-root convention and both versions of Viete's theorem are correct in their stated context."
        },
        {
          "id": "n46-prose-4",
          "source_refs": ["p0275#7"],
          "derivation": "x^2-7x+10=(x-2)(x-5); D=49-40=9; sum 7 and product 10.",
          "conclusion": "The monic example is correct."
        },
        {
          "id": "n46-prose-5",
          "source_refs": ["p0275#8", "p0275#9", "p0276#1", "p0276#2", "p0276#3", "p0276#4", "p0276#5", "p0276#6"],
          "derivation": "If m+n=-b/a and mn=c/a with a!=0, then ax^2+bx+c=a[x^2-(m+n)x+mn]=a(x-m)(x-n). The zero-product property proves that the only roots are m,n, including m=n. Division by nonzero a preserves equivalence. The case a=1 follows immediately.",
          "conclusion": "All three numbered equivalent equations, the factorization, the converse, and the 'no other roots' quantifier are correct."
        },
        {
          "id": "n46-prose-6",
          "source_refs": ["p0276#7", "p0276#8", "p0276#9", "p0277#1"],
          "derivation": "Roots 7,-2 have sum 5 and product -14, yielding x^2-5x-14=(x-7)(x+2)=0. Every nonzero scalar multiple has precisely the same roots; infinitely many such equations exist.",
          "conclusion": "The construction and arbitrary nonzero-scalar quantifier are correct."
        }
      ],
      "exercises": [
